patches.behavior:
State per loop index: `{ u: number | null, warned: boolean }`, where `u` is a unit random draw in [0, 1). Starts as `{ u: null, warned: false }`.

Each frame, per loop index:
```
if (ctx.state.u === null || ctx.pulsed("randomize")) ctx.state.u = ctx.services.random();
const s = ctx.input<number>("start");
const e = ctx.input<number>("end");
let v: number;
if (ctx.input<boolean>("wholeNumbers")) {
  const lo = Math.ceil(Math.min(s, e));
  const hi = Math.floor(Math.max(s, e));
  v = hi < lo ? Math.round((s + e) / 2) : lo + Math.floor(ctx.state.u * (hi - lo + 1));
} else {
  v = s + ctx.state.u * (e - s);
}
if (!Number.isFinite(v)) {
  v = 0;
  if (!ctx.state.warned) { ctx.state.warned = true; ctx.services.log("warn", `${ctx.id}: Start or End isn't a finite number, so Random outputs 0`); }
}
if (v === 0) v = 0; // fold -0
ctx.output("value", v);
```

- Pulses: `randomize` is the only pulse input. It fires on an upstream pulse or on a false→true edge of a connected boolean, so a held `true` draws once. Pulses on consecutive frames each draw a new number.
- Frame 0: each loop index draws once on its first evaluation. A `randomize` pulse on that same frame doesn't draw a second time.
- Range: the held draw `u` is rescaled every frame. Changing Start or End moves Value proportionally without a new draw; this includes a range that arrives a frame late from layer-derived outputs. Decimal mode covers [Start, End) when Start < End and (End, Start] when reversed, and Start == End outputs Start. Whole-number mode is inclusive at both ends and ignores which input is larger. When no whole number lies in the range (Start 0.2, End 0.8), it outputs the whole number nearest the middle.
- Determinism: draws come only from `ctx.services.random()`, never `Math.random`. The engine evaluates loop indices in ascending order, so for a given seed and input sequence the draw order, and therefore every value, is reproducible. Draw at most once per index per frame.
- Loops: per-index state; each index keeps its own draw. A scalar Randomize pulse broadcasts, so every index draws a new number on the same frame. New indices draw on their first evaluation; removed indices drop their state.
- Edge cases: never outputs NaN or ±Infinity. Warn once per restart per index through `ctx.services.log("warn", ...)`.
- Restart and dispose: state resets to `{ u: null, warned: false }`, so frame 0 draws again from the reseeded stream. Nothing to release.
- Coming from Origami: Origami doesn't document the value before the first pulse. Sonobe draws on the first evaluation so looped layouts scatter without a pulse. Origami also doesn't document what happens when Start or End change between pulses; Sonobe rescales the current draw. `wholeNumbers` is a Sonobe addition; importers leave it false.

State per loop index: `{ warned: false }`, used only to log the non-finite warning once. Values are stateless.

Each frame, per loop index:
```
const a = ctx.input<number>("angle") % 360;       // exact in floating point
let v = Math.sin(a * Math.PI / 180);
v = Math.round(v * 1e12) / 1e12;                   // removes float fuzz; identical in every JS engine
if (!Number.isFinite(v)) {
  v = 0;
  if (!ctx.state.warned) { ctx.state.warned = true; ctx.services.log("warn", `${ctx.id}: Angle isn't a finite number, so Sine outputs 0`); }
}
if (v === 0) v = 0;                                // fold -0
ctx.output("output", v);
```

- Precision: reducing the angle with `%` before converting keeps results accurate for huge angles, such as hours of Time × 360. Rounding to 12 decimal places gives exact results at common angles (`sine(180)` is exactly 0, `sine(30)` exactly 0.5, `sine(270)` exactly -1) and hides last-bit differences between browsers' `Math.sin`.
- Frame 0: evaluates with the authored angle; no history.
- Loops: once per loop index; the output length follows the Angle loop.
- Edge cases: only a non-finite Angle (±Infinity or NaN from a bad upstream value) can produce a non-finite result. Output 0 and warn once per restart per index through `ctx.services.log("warn", ...)`.
- Restart and dispose: restart clears `warned`; nothing to release.
- Coming from Origami: Origami's Sine page doesn't state units. Sonobe uses degrees, matching Origami's documented Arctangent output and layer rotation. `sin()` inside Math Expression uses radians, as in JavaScript.

